#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00e4f805-bb4c-51d9-be19-acc3021667c1" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Order of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "9b140d05-1f2d-5415-8110-5bf3b3d26a15" :question/math-academy-id "e-21710" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x$, show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$." :question/worked-solution "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a$, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & g(x) &} \\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < & x-a\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & x &} \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < \\mid x\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta}\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & &= & -3x^{4} + x^{2} + 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\le\\begin{vmatrix}3x^{4} & + & x^{2} & + & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid x \\mid < 1$, then we have ${\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^2 \\lt & x\\end{vmatrix}}$. Also, since $\\mid x \\mid <1$, we have $\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 < & x\\end{vmatrix}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 \\le 3 & x & \\quad\\text{and}\\quad & x & ^2 \\le & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\mid f(x) \\mid \\mid \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & + & x & + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; = 8 \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq 8 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhenever $\\mid x \\mid < 1$. Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and\n\n$$\n{\\delta = 1}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the definition, and we conclude that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(x)\n$$\n\nas $x\\to 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/id #uuid "b1cfa7a9-d7d7-53d5-9ae8-898f0c4e83df" :question/math-academy-id "e-21715" :question/problem "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x$, then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$.\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines $XXXXXXXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$ property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $XXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The constant multiple property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The reflexive property.\n$f = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The transitive property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The absorption property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L3}$. From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n$e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$,\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n$\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}}$, we obtain\n$f = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n$f = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1$, it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n$f = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/id #uuid "6fcba869-b80e-5aa4-bdcc-4bd999fb1dc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7762" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n2. $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n3. $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty$. To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x$. Then, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}$.\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$, we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1}; = 1 + lim_(x → ∞)(e^{x} + \\ln x); = 1 + ∞; = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((\\ln x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1/x}; = lim_(x → ∞)x(e^{x} + \\ln x + 1); = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{xe^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x} + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = 0 + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)((\\ln x)^{′})/((e^{x})^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1/x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{xe^{x}}; = 0$.\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00e4f805-bb4c-51d9-be19-acc3021667c1" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Order of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9713c579-44cf-56ee-aaa4-7bcf28aedb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "real" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155294} {:answer/id #uuid "d072698b-8c80-5118-be51-462cb88e3590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155295}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b67bad92-6e03-5889-ada9-476e2e52e9a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ff49f5-5b8e-5664-802d-67b8e048dc2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|f(x)|≥ K|g(x)|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155297} {:answer/id #uuid "05f3df61-2f8e-5d0f-925f-a6aec1d15983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|f(x)|≤ K|g(x)|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155298} {:answer/id #uuid "6016b9ed-e383-53a9-8af9-42a781ba1081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ≥ Kg(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155299} {:answer/id #uuid "792cda6a-0a66-5865-90e2-3b8cbc85a8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) ≤ Kg(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155300}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0e305b-65b2-5b47-81bb-3a7f64510c04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155296}] :question/id #uuid "58ead12e-e4e3-55bb-ac14-4fbcc74984c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334055" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = 8x^{4} - 2x + 1$, show that $f = O(1)$ as $x → 0$.We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x → a$, and we write\n$f(x) = O(g(x))asx → a$\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $δ$ such that\n\n$f(x) \\le Kg(x)$ {{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b326f7d-4b2c-5d3e-a4a2-86e3c9f76e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155341} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdd90db-c0a9-5e9c-80cf-bf2f97247385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155342} {:answer/id #uuid "c94bd940-f361-5973-ba21-380107c0f4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155343} {:answer/id #uuid "39702b3c-c244-524d-8ac1-c4fe87ac4b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155344} {:answer/id #uuid "3d53d82f-c35e-52c6-8873-f6d52c2e56af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfdd90db-c0a9-5e9c-80cf-bf2f97247385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155342} :answer-field/id #uuid "31db3fd7-9e37-53bf-8ed3-31e1cc5933df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155340}] :question/id #uuid "c0a1373b-0f2f-546e-8300-5cb2dc8f926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334693" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x) = x + 2e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f = O(x)$ as $x → ∞$\n2. $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$\n3. $f = O(e^{3x})$ as $x → ∞$"}]}
